    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado's 2026 legislative session treated AI as a subject of regulation across health care, psychotherapy, conversational services, and automated decision-making, not as a single technology bill.\n\nThe pattern is breadth over depth. Thirty-two bills touch data, privacy, surveillance, utilities, and AI directly, and the AI-specific ones split by application: SB051 on age attestation, HB1195 on psychotherapy, HB1139 on health care, HB1263 on conversational service operators, SB189 on automated decision-making. Colorado is legislating AI by domain rather than by model or compute.\n\nTwo bills connect AI to physical infrastructure. SB102 on large-load data centers and HB1030 on data center and utility modernization sit alongside HB1326 sunsetting the Public Utilities Commission and HB1246 on consumer-regulated electric utilities. That is the grid thread, and it is thin here: the titles show the legislature is looking at large loads and utility structure, but the data carries no capacity figures, no interconnection numbers, and no indication of what any bill would require.\n\nThe news items are two, and one is a city-level move in Linden to prohibit AI data centers. That is a local zoning action, not state policy, and it is the only item in the set that names a specific facility restriction. Nothing here reports bill outcomes, votes, or effective dates, so the list shows what was introduced or dated, not what passed.",
